msgid "wait.h"
msgstr "wait.h"

msgid "Optional sequence-latched waiting without hidden workers."
msgstr "Optional sequence-latched waiting without hidden workers."

msgid "Wait for an authoritative caller predicate."
msgstr "Wait for an authoritative caller predicate."

msgid "Single-waiter notification port."
msgstr "Single-waiter notification port."

msgid "Caller owns storage and serializes arm/wait. Multiple wake publishers are permitted by each backend's documented context. Sequence is a hint; the predicate/request is authoritative. There must be fewer than 2^32 wakes between arm and recheck. All deadlines are absolute monotonic microseconds in the backend's clock domain, with NX_DEADLINE_NEVER explicitly disabling timeout. Clocks must continue while waiting and never change domain; construct relative budgets with nx_deadline_after to avoid overflow. A timeout does not settle a request or release its buffers. Before destroy, stop and join every publisher and waiter; SETTLED alone does not prove notification storage is reclaimable. arm snapshots the sequence. wait must atomically observe a changed sequence or a latched wake, including a wake between the caller's recheck and sleeping. A changed sequence cannot bypass a finite deadline: wait checks expiry on every call and every return from blocking before reporting a hint. A nonblocking backend may return BUSY while unexpired. Only one owner may arm/wait; a second concurrent wait is outside the port contract even when a backend rejects it."
msgstr "Caller owns storage and serializes arm/wait. Multiple wake publishers are permitted by each backend's documented context. Sequence is a hint; the predicate/request is authoritative. There must be fewer than 2^32 wakes between arm and recheck. All deadlines are absolute monotonic microseconds in the backend's clock domain, with NX_DEADLINE_NEVER explicitly disabling timeout. Clocks must continue while waiting and never change domain; construct relative budgets with nx_deadline_after to avoid overflow. A timeout does not settle a request or release its buffers. Before destroy, stop and join every publisher and waiter; SETTLED alone does not prove notification storage is reclaimable. arm snapshots the sequence. wait must atomically observe a changed sequence or a latched wake, including a wake between the caller's recheck and sleeping. A changed sequence cannot bypass a finite deadline: wait checks expiry on every call and every return from blocking before reporting a hint. A nonblocking backend may return BUSY while unexpired. Only one owner may arm/wait; a second concurrent wait is outside the port contract even when a backend rejects it."
